\title{Diagonalizing $A = S\Lambda S^{-1}$ via deflation}
\author{}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\section{Problem Statement}
Given a linear transformation matrix $A$. We want to know:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Is $A$ diagonalizable as $S\Lambda S^{-1}$?
    \item If so, how to find $S$ matrix and $\Lambda$ matrix which contain only eigenvalues on the diagonal?
\end{itemize}

\section{Core idea of deflation}
Assuming $A$ is diagonalizable, we can't just find all the eigenvalues directly, and find the eigenbasis of each eigenvalue and assemble these bases to $S$. The assembled $S$ is still invertible as all these bases are linearly independent. The problem lies in how to find all the eigenvalues.

Say we try to solve $\det(A - \lambda I) = 0$ as a polynomial of $\lambda$. The problem is that when the polynomial is above 5th degree, it was proven not solvable.

That is why we need to use power iteration method and deflation to keep finding all the eigenvalues one by one.

The core idea of deflation is simple, just like SVD decomposition, we can view $A$ as sums of outer products of eigen:
\[
A = \sum_{i=1}^{k} \lambda_i S_i Q_i^T
\]
Here, $S_i$ is the matrix as $\lambda_i$ eigenspace basis. $Q_i^T$ can be deemed as $S_i^+$. Thus $Q_i^T S_i = I_{p_i \times p_i}$. Here $p_i$ is the dim of $E_{\lambda_i}$.

Once we use power iteration method to find dominant $\lambda_1$, we can use RREF to find basis of $(A - \lambda_1 I)$ null space. The basis is $S_1$.

We can use left inverse of $S_1$, aka $S_1^+$, aka $(S_1^T S_1)^{-1} S_1^T$ as $Q_1^T$. Then we calculate:
\[
A_{\text{deflated}} = A - \lambda_1 S_1 Q_1^T
\]
With $A_{\text{deflated}}$, we can keep going to find and subtract next outer product.

Note, here, for each $\lambda_i$, we find the $S_i$. If we assemble $S = (S_1 \dots S_k)$, the columns of $S$ are linearly independent. The idea is simple, because we can view the input space $\mathbb{R}^n$ as:
\[
\mathbb{R}^n = E_{\lambda_1} \oplus E_{\lambda_2} \oplus \dots \oplus E_{\lambda_k}
\]
The $k$ eigenspaces are kind of like linearly independent vectors in the sense any vector $\vec{v}$ in $\mathbb{R}^n$ has only one unique decomposition in these $k$ eigenspaces. Since inside the eigenspace $E_{\lambda_i}$, $S_i$ matrix also contains basis as its columns. It is easy to imagine two steps process to prove $S$ columns are linearly independent.

By now we have $S$ and $\Lambda$. However, one thing to note is that the $Q_i^T$ can't be assembled as $Q^T = (Q_1^T \ Q_2^T \ \dots \ Q_k^T)^T$. $Q^T S \neq I$ in general. This is because we are using orthogonal left inverse to find $Q_i^T$. More on this later as we will dig into oblique projection, dual Gram-Schmidt process later. For now, we can just calculate the $Q^T$ as $S^{-1}$ using RREF.

\section{Core idea of testing if $A$ is diagonalizable}
If $A$ is diagonalizable depends on if each eigenspace's geometrical dimension is the same as the eigenvalue's algebraic multiplicity.

But how do we know once we get eigenvalue from power method?

Let us look at the more general Canonical Jordan decomposition of a linear transformation $A$. Our diagonalizable matrix is just a special form of CJM. From CJM point of view, the input $\mathbb{R}^n$ is direct sum of extended eigenspace:
\[
\mathbb{R}^n = T_1 \oplus T_2 \oplus \dots \oplus T_k
\]
$T_i$ here is extended eigenspace.

So what is extended eigenspace $T_\lambda$? The following graph illustrates well:

% --- ENHANCED & LARGER TIKZ DIAGRAM ---
\vspace{0.5cm}
\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \begin{tikzpicture}[
        ellipse node/.style={draw, ellipse, thick},
        layer1/.style={ellipse node, draw=blue!80!black, fill=blue!8, minimum width=3.8cm, minimum height=2.2cm},
        layer2/.style={ellipse node, draw=blue!60!black, fill=blue!3, minimum width=6.8cm, minimum height=4.2cm},
        layerM/.style={ellipse node, draw=black, minimum width=9.8cm, minimum height=6.2cm}
    ]
    
    % Outermost ellipse ker(A - \lambda I)^m
    \node (kerM) [layerM] {};
    
    % Middle ellipse ker(A - \lambda I)^2
    \node (ker2) [layer2] {};
    
    % Innermost ellipse ker(A - \lambda I)
    \node (ker1) [layer1] {\large $\ker(A - \lambda I)$};
    
    % Text Labels in gaps
    \node [font=\large] at (0, 1.55) {$\ker(A - \lambda I)^2$};
    \node [font=\Large] at (0, 2.30) {$\vdots$};
    \node [font=\large] at (0, 2.75) {$\ker(A - \lambda I)^m$};

    % Arrow and Multiplicity Label on the Right
    \draw [-{Stealth[scale=1.2]}, line width=1.2pt, color=red!70!black] 
        (kerM.east) -- ++(1.8cm, 0) 
        node[right, align=left, font=\large] {$\dim$ is $\lambda$'s\\[3pt]algebraic multiplicity};
    
    \end{tikzpicture}
    \caption*{Nested Extended Eigenspaces (The multi-layer onion)}
\end{figure}
\vspace{0.5cm}

Obviously:
\[
\ker(A - \lambda I) \subseteq \ker(A - \lambda I)^2 \subseteq \dots \subseteq \ker(A - \lambda I)^m = \ker(A - \lambda I)^{m+1}
\]
It is like a multi-layer onion. The innermost layer is $\ker(A - \lambda I)$ aka $E_\lambda$.

If we keep expanding, at one point $\ker(A - \lambda I)^m = \ker(A - \lambda I)^{m+1}$. Because the dim of $A$ is limited, it must stop somewhere.

For diagonalizable $A$, all the extended eigenspace $T$ is the same as $E_\lambda$. Put it another way:
\[
\ker(A - \lambda I) = \ker(A - \lambda I)^2
\]
This is the test we are looking for.

